  "id": "DrugBank_DB01275 - MONDO_5044",
  "verbalization": "Hydralazine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hypertensive Disorder through its role in treating Hypertension, which in turn is linked to Hypertensive Disorder. The evidence suggests that Hydralazine exerts its therapeutic effect on Hypertension via mechanisms that are broadly associated with antihypertensive action, such as vasodilation and reduction of vascular resistance. This connection is supported by the shared indication of Hypertension in all three paths, with each of the downstream drugs (Xipamide, Rilmenidine, and Trimazosin) also being indicated for Hypertension, and each of these drugs being associated with Hypertensive Disorder. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a strong, albeit indirect, relationship between Hydralazine and Hypertensive Disorder, likely mediated through the treatment of Hypertension. The overlapping pathways suggest that the mechanism of action for Hydralazine may involve similar physiological effects that contribute to the development of Hypertensive Disorder, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
